  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 1:  # direct project beneficiaries disaggregated by sex (individual people)
Indicator 2 (GEF Core Indicators 9): Reduction, disposal/destruction, phase out, elimination and avoidance of chemicals of global concern and their waste in the environment and in processes, materials and products [Metric Tonnes]
Indicator 3 (GEF Core Indicators 9.6): Quantity of POPs/Mercury containing materials and products directly avoided [Metric Tonnes]
Indicator 4: Number of customs officials and environmental enforcement officers as well as the number of environmental/inspection agencies of whom the capacity has been further improved to ensure the adequate monitoring/ enforcement of (future) import/use bans in place.
Indicator 5: Number of centralized facilities for the safe interim storage and export of chemicals and hazardous wastes established. 
Indicator 6: Number of tonnes of hazardous wastes that cannot be treated in the country, have been exported and soundly treated. 
Indicator 7: Number of circular systems for waste management improved. 
Indicator 8: Number of economic instruments designed and submitted for approval.  
Indicator 9: Number of gender-responsive ISLANDS knowledge products, which capture best practices and technologies on the sound management chemicals and waste for SIDS, published and shared at national, regional and global level to raise awareness and share best practices.
Indicator 10: All required UNDP/GEF M&amp;E and adaptive management processes applied.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2021">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Based on export data from customs, the only types of hazardous wastes being exported are waste oils and car batteries, although quantities fluctuate considerably per year. On average (2014 &#x2013; 2018), 185 tonnes of car batteries and 73 tonnes of waste oil were exported on a yearly basis for recycling and treatment abroad. 

Any other types of hazardous waste are not being treated/exported but are disposed of at municipal waste dumpsites or stored until disposal solutions will have been identified. 

The global e-waste statistics partnership estimates that yearly 2,500 tonnes of e-waste are being generated in the Maldives. As e-waste (for now) has the same HS code as scrap metal, no data is available on the amount of e-waste being collected and exported. 

The Maldives Customs Service (MCS) has a training academy which conduct trainings periodically in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment on CITES and the identification of ODS.

The Ministry of Defence (MoD) maintains a Central Chemical Management System (CCMS) called &#x201c;MAKUDI&#x201d; which will be operational starting February 2020. CCMS maintains records of all chemical imports and was developed with SAICM support.

The only hazardous waste inventory that has been conducted so far has been the NIP POPs inventory which was compiled in 2016. The NIP inventory did not cover any other hazardous wastes generated in the country. Hence, currently no data on hazardous waste generation and/or stockpiles are available. 

A municipal waste assessment is being conducted under an ADB funded project (2018 - 2023).

A Chemicals Regulation was adopted/approved in 2019 and contains aspects related to: a) Labelling and import of chemicals; b) Sales of Chemicals; c) Storage of Chemicals; d) Transport of Chemicals throughout the country.

Waste Management Regulations (2012) are in place under the Environmental Protection and Preservation Act (1992), which covers some aspects related to hazardous waste management. 

